<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="misread">Common misunderstandings</h2>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Read it right</span><b>It's a catalog, not a single story</b> — Don't expect a plot. The Shan Hai Jing is a field guide — a 2,000-year-old atlas of mountains, seas, gods and beasts, region by region. It's the source material, not the movie.</div>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Read it right</span><b>The creatures aren't all evil monsters</b> — Many are auspicious, protective or simply strange. Some bring floods or fire; others ward off disease. 'Monster' is the wrong lens — these are the building blocks of a mythical world.</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="faq">The Classic of Mountains and Seas FAQ</h2>
  <div class="note" style="margin:10px 0"><b>What is the Classic of Mountains and Seas?</b><p style="margin:6px 0 0">The 山海经 (Shān Hǎi Jīng) is an ancient Chinese text — part geography, part bestiary, part myth — cataloging hundreds of mountains, lands, gods and fantastical creatures. It's the original source for much of Chinese mythology's monster lore.</p></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin:10px 0"><b>How old is it?</b><p style="margin:6px 0 0">Its layers date roughly from the Warring States period to the Han dynasty — over 2,000 years old — compiled by many unknown hands rather than a single author.</p></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin:10px 0"><b>Is it a story or a reference book?</b><p style="margin:6px 0 0">A reference book. It reads like a surveyor's notes on an impossible world: this mountain, that river, the god who lives there, the beast you'll meet and what it means if you do.</p></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin:10px 0"><b>Is any of it real?</b><p style="margin:6px 0 0">It mixes real geography with pure myth. Some places and animals are exaggerated versions of real ones; others are entirely fantastical. It's best read as a map of the ancient Chinese imagination.</p></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin:10px 0"><b>What famous creatures come from it?</b><p style="margin:6px 0 0">The nine-tailed fox, the taotie (a gluttonous mouth-beast), winged dragons, and many gods and hybrid animals that still appear in films, games and art today.</p></div>
</section>

